NAME
    pack - compact the volume, reclaiming dead sectors

SYNOPSIS
    pack
    pack -v
    pack -h

DESCRIPTION
    Files and directories are single contiguous extents, and new ones
    are only ever allocated at the volume's free pointer, so a sector
    that stops being used stays dead: a deleted or replaced file, the
    pipes' /PIPE0.TMP and /PIPE1.TMP, the old copy a >> append leaves
    when the file was not the last one written, a removed directory.
    pack moves every live file and directory down over those holes,
    in disk order, updates each directory entry to the new place, and
    lowers the free pointer in the boot block. It then prints:

        pack: 9 sectors reclaimed, free pointer 645 (was 654)
        pack: 64 files and 5 directories moved

    The current directory stays current (pack re-enters it by its
    path, since the directory itself may have moved), and the shell
    re-reads the free pointer when pack returns, so the next file is
    written at the new free pointer. Each directory's '.' and '..'
    are checked and rewritten where they are wrong. Deleted entries
    stay in their directories as tombstones: the OS reuses them for
    new entries, so no room is lost there.

    pack refuses to run with a < or > or >> or inside a pipe: those
    are files the shell holds open, and a file open for writing grows
    at the old free pointer. It refuses a volume whose extents
    overlap or reach past the free pointer, before writing anything
    (check such a card with tools/p8xfs.py fsck on the host).
    pack is /BIN/PACK.

OPTIONS
    -v  one line per moved extent: "from>to", or "from>to via S" for
        a two-step move through the scratch area at LBA S, then a
        letter as each step is done: c copied to the scratch area,
        e entry on the scratch copy, C copied to its place, E entry
        on the final copy.

RESET-SAFE
    A reset or power loss while pack runs loses nothing, and running
    pack again finishes the job. At every moment every directory
    entry points at a complete copy of its file or directory:
      - An extent whose hole is at least its own size is copied into
        the hole, and only then is its entry pointed at the copy (one
        sector write); until then the old copy is untouched.
      - An extent whose hole is smaller would overwrite the start of
        its own old copy, so it moves in two steps: copied whole to a
        scratch area right above the free pointer, its entry pointed
        there, copied down to its place, its entry pointed there.
        Before the first move the free pointer on the card is raised
        over the scratch area, so a copy parked there is part of the
        volume if pack stops.
      - A directory's '.' and its subdirectories' '..' follow at once,
        and every '.' and '..' is checked and repaired on each run.
    An interrupted pack leaves every file intact, and the next pack
    reclaims what is left, the scratch area included. One window is
    known: a cut during a two-step directory move can leave a
    subdirectory's '..' pointing at the scratch copy (fsck complains);
    no file is lost, and running pack again repairs it. (Tested on the
    emulator by cutting pack off at 120 points in every phase: nothing
    was lost, and every rerun left a clean volume.) The
    one thing no program can guard against is a sector half-written
    when the power drops, as with any write.

LIMITS
    800 files and directories on the volume. Only sectors behind the
    first hole are copied, one sector at a time, and a two-step move
    copies twice: a pack after deleting a small file near the start
    of the disk moves nearly everything, mostly in two steps.
    The scratch area needs as many free sectors above the free pointer
    as the largest two-step file or directory. The volume does not
    record its size, so pack reads the last scratch sector first: a
    card refuses a sector past its end, and pack then stops with
    "no room on the card above the free pointer" before writing
    anything. Nothing on the volume can lie past LBA 65535 (32 MB).

EXAMPLES
    del /OLD.TXT
    pack
    dir

NOTES
    New in Y1/OS (2026-09-23). On the P8X, PACK was a shell built-in;
    here it is a program, working on raw sectors through the ROM's
    CFREAD/CFWRITE, so it costs the OS nothing.

SEE ALSO
    del, rmdir, fs
